course: dictionary
lesson: 2914
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one the writer wants. The word drag is a good example. «Toad is dragged off in chains, shrieking, across the market-place» means pulled along the ground by force. «A kind surprise should not drag on too long» means going slowly, not being pulled at all.
explain: Look at the words around drag to find the meaning. Chains and dungeon tell you it is about pulling. Words like long and on tell you it is about time. Try your meaning in the sentence: if «it goes on too slowly» fits, you have the right one.
explain: Drag can also be a noun. «What a drag» means something boring or annoying. In science, drag is a force that slows things down as they move through air or water. Streamlined shapes, like fish and racing bikes, have less drag, and parachutes use lots of drag on purpose.
fact: Drag can be a verb (to drag something) or a noun (a drag), so check which one the sentence needs.
fact: «Drag» means to pull something along, often because it is heavy.
fact: «Drag on» means to go on for too long and feel slow.
fact: In science, drag is a force that slows things down in air or water, and streamlined shapes have less drag.
fact: On a computer, to drag means to move something on the screen by holding the mouse button, or your finger, and moving it.
exercise: «A walrus climbing out onto the ice drags its heavy body up.» What does drags mean here? | Shouts loudly | Sleeps on the ice | Swims very fast | *Pulls along with effort, because it is heavy
exercise: Nadia says, «This lesson is a drag.» What does drag mean? | A kind of boat | Something that glows | Something exciting and fun | *Something boring or annoying
exercise: In science, what does drag do to a moving object? | *Slows it down as it moves through air or water | Makes it heavier for ever | Makes it glow | Turns it into a fish
test: On a computer, «drag and drop» means | Closing a file with a click | *Holding the mouse button down and moving something | Shouting at the screen | Pulling a file with the mouse button held down, then letting go of it in a new place
test: Which shape has the least drag when it moves through air? | A flat square board | A tangled ball of wire | A parachute | *A streamlined fish shape
exam: «No talk left in you, and your feet dragging like lead» means you are | Happy and excited to be walking | Full of energy and ready to run | Hungry and cold | *Very tired, with heavy, slow feet
